## 3) How We Made It Parallel

**Input split:** Instead of processing one vertex at a time, we process all vertices at the same distance (the "current frontier") together. If the frontier has 8 vertices and we have 4 workers, each worker gets 2 vertices.

**What each worker does:** Each worker looks at its assigned vertices and copies their neighbor lists. This is a read-only operation—no conflicts possible.

**Where outputs go:** Each worker writes to its own slot in a pre-allocated array (per_vertex_neighbors[i]). Workers never write to each other's slots.

**Fixed-order merge:** After all workers finish, we sequentially scan the per-vertex neighbor lists in frontier order (vertex 0's neighbors, then vertex 1's neighbors, etc.). As we scan, we check if each neighbor has been visited. If not, we mark it visited and add it to the next frontier. This sequential scan ensures deterministic ordering.

so the output is the same as the sequential bfs.

the way it is working is, if the ertices is less than 100 it will fallback to the sequential bfs.
Otherwise it will use level-synchronous bfs to parallelize the bfs. the good thing about this
implementation is it will respect the sequential bfs order by using per_vertex_neighbors to store
the neighbors of each vertex in the current frontier, and then merging them in order to create the next frontier.

the sequnetial: 59.293754 ms/run ± 5.614498
the parallel: 82.301318 ms/run ± 5.851854

The impelemntation sound correct and deterministic, but the performance is not good as the sequential one,
this is because of the overhead of thread creation and synchronization,

The parallelized portion only copies neighbor lists to per-vertex buffers, which is a trivial operation. The actual
BFS work—checking and updating the visited set—runs sequentially after the parallel section and dominates runtime.
Thread coordination overhead (spawning, synchronizing, dynamic scheduling with chunk size 1) exceeds the cost of the
parallelized work, while the sequential bottleneck remains unchanged. Parallelizing the neighbor copy adds overhead
without reducing the bottleneck.
